hadiths[14]{hadithnumber,text}:
"27708","Haddathana Sufyan from Amr from al-Zuhri from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father, raising it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning that the souls of the martyrs are in green birds eating from the fruits of Paradise; and it was read to Sufyan: a soul eating in the fruits or trees of Paradise."
"27709","Haddathana Abu Muawiya who said: Haddathana Hisham ibn Urwa from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat with three fingers and would not wipe his hand until he licked it."
"27710","Haddathana Abu Muawiya who said: Haddathana al-Hajjaj from Nafi from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father that a black slave girl of theirs slaughtered a sheep with a marwa stone, so Kab mentioned it to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he ordered him to eat it."
"27711","Haddathana Ibn Numayr from Hisham from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sad that Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik or Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik informed him from his father Kab that he told them that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to eat with three fingers, and when he finished he licked them."
"27712","Haddathana Abu Usama who said: Akhbarana Ibn Jurayj from al-Zuhri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not return from a journey except in the forenoon, so he would begin with the mosque and pray two rakas in it and sit in it."
"27713","Haddathana Yazid and Abu al-Nadr who said: Anba al-Masudi from Sad ibn Ibrahim from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The parable of the believer is like the tender plant of cultivation which the winds bend, laying it down at one time and straightening it at another, until his term comes to him; and the parable of the disbeliever is like the arzah tree standing firm on its root, nothing shakes it, until its uprooting is at once."
"27714","Haddathana Muhammad ibn Bakr and Abd al-Razzaq who said: Anba Ibn Jurayj who said: Haddathani Ibn Shihab that Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Kab said, Abd al-Razzaq said: and from his uncle Ubayd Allah ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not return from a journey except in the daytime in the forenoon, and when he returned he began with the mosque and prayed two rakas in it, then sat in it."
"27715","Haddathana Surayj and Abu Jafar al-Madaini who said: Haddathana Abbad from Sufyan ibn Husayn from al-Zuhri from Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by him while he was clinging to a man, so he said: What is this; he said: O Messenger of Allah, a debtor of mine; and he gestured with his hand to take half; I said: O Messenger of Allah, yes; he said: So he took half and left half."
"27716","Haddathana Abd al-Razzaq who said: Akhbarana Mamar from al-Zuhri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father that he said to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Indeed Allah, mighty and majestic, has revealed concerning poetry what He revealed; so he said: Indeed the believer strives with his sword and his tongue; and by the One in whose hand is my soul, it is as if what you shoot them with is a shower of arrows."
"27717","Haddathana Abd al-Razzaq who said: Haddathana Mamar from al-Zuhri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father who said: I did not stay behind from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a battle he fought until the battle of Tabuk, except Badr; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not blame anyone who stayed behind from Badr; he only went out seeking the caravan, and Quraysh went out to aid their caravan, and they met without appointment as Allah, mighty and majestic, said; and by my life, indeed the noblest of the scenes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people is Badr, and I would not like that I had witnessed it in place of my pledge on the night of al-Aqaba where we agreed upon Islam; and I did not stay behind after from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a battle he fought until the battle of Tabuk, and it is the last battle he fought; so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) permitted the people to depart and wanted them to prepare the preparation of their battle, and that was when the shades were pleasant and the fruits were ripe; and he would hardly intend a battle except he alluded to another, and Yaqub said from Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab: except he alluded with other than it; Haddathanahu Sufyan from Mamar from al-Zuhri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik, and he said in it: he alluded to other than it; then he returned to the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq; and he used to say: War is deceit; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended in the battle of Tabuk that the people prepare preparation; and I was at my easiest when I had gathered two mounts, and I was most able in myself for jihad and lightness of baggage, and in that I inclined to the shades and ripeness of fruits; and I remained so until the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose departing in the morning, and that was Thursday, and he liked to go out on Thursday, so he set out in the morning; so I said: I will go tomorrow to the market and buy my gear then join them; so I went to the market the next day and some of my affair was difficult for me, so I returned; so I said: I will return tomorrow, Allah willing, and join them; and some of my affair was difficult for me; and I remained so until sin enveloped me and I stayed behind from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); so I walked in the markets and circled Madina, and it grieved me that I saw no one who stayed behind except a man accused of hypocrisy; and there was none who stayed behind except he thought that would be hidden for him; and the people were many, no register gathered them; and all who stayed behind from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were some eighty men; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not mention me until he reached Tabuk; and when he reached Tabuk he said: What did Kab ibn Malik do; so a man of my people said: His two cloaks kept him behind, O Messenger of Allah, and looking at his sides; and Yaqub said from Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab: his two cloaks and looking at his sides; so Muadh ibn Jabal said: Evil is what you said; by Allah, O Prophet of Allah, we know only good; and while they were so, there they were with a man whom the mirage moves; so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be Abu Khaythama; and behold it was Abu Khaythama; and when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed the battle of Tabuk and returned and drew near Madina, I began to remember how I escape the anger of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I sought help for that from every man of judgment of my family, until when it was said: The Prophet will come to you in the morning, falsehood left me and I knew I will not be saved except with truth; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered in the forenoon and prayed in the mosque two rakas, and when he came from travel he did that, and he entered the mosque and prayed two rakas then sat; and those who stayed behind began coming to him, swearing to him and excusing to him, so he sought forgiveness for them and accepted their outward and entrusted their inner to Allah, mighty and majestic; so I entered the mosque and behold he was sitting; and when he saw me he smiled the smile of one angry; so I came and sat before him; so he said: Had you not bought a mount; I said: Yes, O Prophet of Allah; he said: So what kept you behind; I said: By Allah, had I sat before anyone of the people other than you I would have escaped his anger with an excuse, I have been given argument; and Yaqub said from Ibn Akhi Ibn Shihab: I would have seen to escape his anger with an excuse; and in the hadith of Uqayl: I escape his anger with an excuse; and in it: Allah will soon make Him angry with you upon me; and if I tell you a true story you find against me in it, indeed I hope in it the pardon of Allah; then he returned to the hadith of Abd al-Razzaq; but I knew, O Prophet of Allah, that if I inform you today of a word you find against me in it, and it is true, I hope in it the pardon of Allah; and if I tell you today a story you are pleased with me in it, and it is a lie, Allah will soon expose me to you; and by Allah, O Prophet of Allah, I was never easier nor lighter of baggage than when I stayed behind from you; so he said: As for this, he has told you truth; stand until Allah decrees concerning you; so I stood, and men of my people rose after me blaming me, and they said: By Allah, we do not know you sinned a sin ever before this, so why did you not excuse to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with an excuse he is pleased with you in, and the seeking of forgiveness of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would have come after your sin, and you did not place yourself a stand you do not know what is decreed for you in it; and they kept blaming me until I intended to return and belie myself; so I said: Did anyone else say this word; they said: Yes, Hilal ibn Umayya and Murara, meaning Ibn Rabia; so they mentioned two righteous men who witnessed Badr, for me in them, meaning, an example; so I said: By Allah, I will never return to him in this nor belie myself; and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the people from speaking to us, we three; he said: So I went out to the market and none spoke to me, and the people became strange to us until they were not those we know, and the walls we know became strange to us until they were not the walls we know, and the earth became strange to us until it was not the earth we know; and I was strongest of my companions, so I went out and circled the markets and came to the mosque and entered and came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and greeted him, and I said: Did he move his lips with greeting; and when I stood praying toward a pillar and turned to my prayer he looked at me with the corner of his eye, and when I looked at him he turned from me; and my two companions submitted and wept night and day not raising their heads; and while I was circling the market, a Christian man came with food selling it, saying: Who guides to Kab ibn Malik; so the people began pointing to me; so he came to me and brought me a letter of the king of Ghassan, and in it: As for after, it has reached me that your companion has forsaken you and banished you, and you are not in a land of waste nor humiliation, so join us we will console you; so I said: This also is of trial and evil, so I heated the oven for it and burnt it in it; and when forty nights passed, a messenger of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me and said: Keep away from your wife; so I said: Shall I divorce her; he said: No, but do not approach her; so the wife of Hilal came and said: O Messenger of Allah, indeed Hilal ibn Umayya is a weak old man, so do you permit me to serve him; he said: Yes, but let him not approach you; she said: O Prophet of Allah, he has no movement for anything; he has remained prostrating weeping night and day since what was of his affair; Kab said: So when the trial lengthened upon me, I leapt upon the wall of Abu Qatada, and he is my cousin, and greeted him, and he did not return upon me; so I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatada, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger; so he was silent; then I said: I adjure you by Allah, O Abu Qatada, do you know that I love Allah and His Messenger; he said: Allah and His Messenger know best; he said: So I could not hold myself but I wept, then I leapt the wall out, until when fifty nights passed from when the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade the people from speaking to us, I prayed upon the back of a house of ours the Fajr prayer then sat, and I was in the state which Allah, mighty and majestic, said: the earth straitened upon us with its wideness and our selves straitened upon us, when I heard a call from the peak of Sal: Glad tidings, O Kab ibn Malik; so I fell prostrate and knew that Allah had brought us relief; then a man came galloping upon a horse to give me tidings, and the voice was faster than his horse; so I gave him my two garments as glad tidings and wore two other garments; and our repentance had descended upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the last third of the night, so Umm Salama said that evening: O Prophet of Allah, shall we not give tidings to Kab ibn Malik; he said: Then the people will crowd you and prevent you sleep rest of night; and Umm Salama was beneficent, reckoning reward, in my affair, grieving at my matter; so I went to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and behold he was sitting in the mosque and around him the Muslims, and he was shining like the shining of the moon, and when he was pleased with a matter he shone; so I came and sat before him; so he said: Glad tidings, O Kab ibn Malik, of the best day that came upon you since the day your mother bore you; I said: O Prophet of Allah, is it from Allah or from you; he said: Rather from Allah, mighty and majestic; then he recited upon them: Allah has turned to the Prophet and the Emigrants and the Helpers, until he reached: Indeed Allah is the Oft-Returning, the Merciful; he said: And concerning us was also revealed: Fear Allah and be with the truthful; so I said: O Prophet of Allah, indeed of my repentance is that I speak only truth and that I strip of all my wealth as charity to Allah, mighty and majestic, and to His Messenger; so he said: Keep some of your wealth, it is better for you; I said: So I keep my share which is at Khaybar; he said: So Allah, mighty and majestic, bestowed upon me no blessing after Islam greater in myself than my truthfulness to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when I told him truth, I and my two companions, that we did not lie and perish as they perished; indeed I hope that Allah, mighty and majestic, did not try anyone in truth like that He tried me; I never intended a lie after, and I hope Allah will preserve me in what remains."
"27718","Haddathana Yahya ibn Adam who said: Haddathana Ibn Mubarak from Mamar and Yunus from al-Zuhri from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik from Kab who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he was pleased, his face would shine until it was as if his face was a piece of the moon; and we used to recognize that in him."
"27719","Haddathana Uthman ibn Umar who said: Akhbarana Yunus from al-Zuhri from Abd Allah ibn Kab ibn Malik that his father informed him that he demanded payment from Ibn Abi Hadrad of a debt he had owed him in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the mosque, and their voices rose until the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) heard them while he was in his house; so he came out to them until he uncovered the curtain of his chamber and called: O Kab ibn Malik; so he said: At your service, O Messenger of Allah; and he gestured to him to remit half of his debt; he said: I have done so, O Messenger of Allah; he said: Stand and pay him."
"27720","Haddathana Ishaq, meaning Ibn al-Tabba, who said: Haddathana Ibn Lahia from Yazid ibn Abi Habib from al-Zuhri from Ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), when he wanted to travel, would not travel except on Thursday."
"27721","Haddathana Hashim who said: Haddathana Abu Mashar from Yazid ibn Khusayfa from Amr ibn Kab ibn Malik from his father who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If one of you finds pain, let him place his hand where he finds his pain, then let him say seven times: I seek refuge in the might of Allah and His power over all things from the evil of what I find."
